\section{The Target Includes the Board}

A hosted compiler can leave much of the address plan to the operating system. For a bare-metal image, the build may need to place a reset vector where the board expects it, allocate stack space in writable memory, and fit executable code into flash. In \emph{Programming Embedded Systems with C and GNU Development Tools}, Michael Barr and Anthony Massa describe cross-compilation, startup, and locating as parts of one build process. Their startup treatment explains a distinction the compiler cannot invent at function entry: initialized writable data may have bytes in the flash image but must reside in RAM when the program uses it. Startup code copies that data and clears zero-initialized storage before entering the high-level runtime.

For example, let a firmware image have 32 KiB of SRAM, with 12 KiB of zero-initialized storage, 7 KiB of initialized writable data, and an 8 KiB reserved stack. At most 5 KiB remains for other allocations before alignment and other reserved sections. The 7 KiB counts against SRAM even though its initial contents are also in flash. Add link-time assertions that the writable sections and reserved stack fit without overlap, and inspect both the load and run addresses in the map. Test on reset with recognizable nonzero values in both RAM regions: startup must preserve the initializers from the image, not the old contents of RAM, and clear the zero-initialized region. An instrumented run under realistic interrupt nesting is a separate check on the assumed stack allowance.

\section{Keep Device Boundaries in the IR}

A heterogeneous target may have a CPU, DSP, or accelerator with separate instructions, memory spaces, and execution queues. Placement can turn an ordinary data edge into an explicit transfer and completion dependency. A buffer's address, alignment, layout, residency, and lifetime all constrain the choice. An asynchronous operation needs a token or equivalent ordering information; issuing a copy is not the same as making its result available. With noncoherent memory, obey the platform's rules for making producer writes visible before the consumer runs. Neither a type cast nor a volatile qualification is a general synchronization or coherence operation.

Li and colleagues' \emph{The Deep Learning Compiler: A Comprehensive Survey} describes tensorization, memory scopes, and cooperative data fetching in accelerator lowering. These expose choices not captured by assigning one target opcode per scalar IR operator. Ahmad and colleagues' \emph{Vector Instruction Selection for Digital Signal Processors using Program Synthesis} likewise separates candidate arithmetic from the lane rearrangements needed to use it. At the level of a heterogeneous graph, a sequence of device regions may profit by keeping a temporary in a device-native layout and transferring only the final result. This also lengthens that temporary's device lifetime, which must fit the memory budget.

\section{Worked Placement: A Transfer Break-Even}

Suppose an independent sample conversion takes $4$ ns per element on a CPU. An accelerator takes $0.8$ ns per element, plus $10$ microseconds for launch and synchronization and transfers of one four-byte input and one four-byte output per element at an effective $8$ GB/s. Ignoring overlap, the transfers cost $1$ ns per element, so the estimated CPU and device times in nanoseconds are
\[
  T_{\rm cpu}(n)=4n,\qquad T_{\rm dev}(n)=10000+1.8n.
\]
The device wins in this model at integer sizes $n\geq4546$. A resident input or a downstream accelerator consumer changes the transfer term; a device unable to hold the chosen batch needs tiling and may pay the launch cost several times. Measure below and above the predicted threshold, including format conversion, scheduling, and the final wait. A kernel-only benchmark cannot validate it.

\section{Validate Placement and Fallback Together}

Require every consumer to see the producer's completed data in the expected address space and layout. A partitioner can check that each cross-device edge has a legal transfer and a completion dependency, that buffer deallocation occurs only after the last outstanding use, and that the fallback obeys the same observable contract. In numerical code, state in advance whether rounding order must match or whether a quantified error bound is part of the contract. Test an unavailable device, zero-length work, and a nonmultiple of the device tile size. The dossier's \href{https://github.com/Chubek/TheTomeOfLangDev/blob/HEAD/dossier-on-modern-cc-design/chapters/25-heterogeneous-ir.md}{Heterogeneous IR} treats this explicit placement and synchronization as a design proposal for larger compiler pipelines.
